The state: the state of the aggregate carrier and, once stretch 3 has made a call, the
state of the loop over the versions. The loop of case 1 uses the whole of `Algo.ADState`;
the plain loop of Eq. (71) uses its first component and keeps the rest at `(0, none)`.

**The raw round `R` implements the mathematical round `ρ`**, with an answer decoder that
may depend on the node.  This is `Algo.Implements` with `decA : ι → α → Set (Equiv.Perm Ω)`;
see the header of this file for why the family is needed.

§15, **the obligations of the master recursion at one degree `n`**, for a round `R` on
master nodes, in the form the lift-free evaluator asks for (`Algo.Round.runQ_eq_answerG`):

* `impl`: at every good node the fold walks the round `ρ P` of the paper (taken at the node's
 own representative), every child being a child in the sense of `Ch`;
* `correct`: `ρ P` returns `Iso^Δ_K(x,y)` of the decoded instance;
* `progress`: the progress of Sec. 11.4 on the raw window size and Johnson parameter, with at
 most `q(n)` calls per round;
* `finish_wf`: the answers the node prints are well formed;
* `root`: the root of every normalized input of degree `n` is a good node.

**Section 15, line 4c on the RAM: the specification.** In the primitive case with
`t ≥ 2` and after line 4b's test has failed at `x`, the node's two lists are the canonical
family of versions of Sec. 14.2, read at `x` and at `y`:

* the family is canonical;
* at `x` it is nonempty and is exactly the set of decoded outcomes of the first list;
* at `y` it is the set of decoded outcomes of the second list when line 4b's test fails at
 `y` too, and empty otherwise (then `H(x)` and `H(y)` have different symmetry defects, so
 `x` and `y` are not `G`-isomorphic);
* it is finite, and at `y` it has at most as many members as the second list;
* every member is a colored `3/4`-partition of `Γ` or a Johnson scheme on at least `3m/4`
 points of `Γ` (`Babai.IsSplitOutcome`).

**Proposition 11.1.6 (Descent), §11.1, what the RAM reduction computes.**  *"Let `H ≤ G`.
Then finding `Iso^Δ_G(x, y)` reduces to `|G : H|` instances of finding `Iso^Δ_H(x, y^σ)` for
various `σ ∈ G`."*  For a well-formed instance `(Δ, G, σ, x, y)` (the coset form of
Remark 11.1.4) and `H ≤ G`, if `ansQ` answers every well-formed instance, then:

* the reduction returns `Iso^Δ_{σG}(x, y)`;
* the number of instances it hands on is `|G : H|`;
* each instance is well formed, has the window `Δ`, the group `H`, and the strings `x, y`
 (so it is `Iso^Δ_{ρH}(x, y) = Iso^Δ_H(x, y^{ρ⁻¹})ρ` by the shift identity, Eq. (59));
* their candidate cosets `ρ_j H` cover `σG`: this is Eq. (62).

The RAM running time is `Algo.proposition_11_1_6_ram`.

**The action data is polynomially small.**  The number of images is the number of
generators, at most `n²`; each image is a permutation of the `t ≤ n` points of the test set;
and the number of cosets is below `(n + t)^(n + t)` (`Algo.size_winCosetIndex_le`).

§14.1 and §15, lines 4d–7a: **the data one round of Procedure Align runs on**.

* `m` and `ϕ` are the giant representation `ϕ : G → S(Γ)` with `Γ = Fin m`;
* `act` is the action of `S(Γ)` on the outcomes of Theorem 9.2.3
 (`Babai.exists_isCatAction_splitOutcomes`);
* `Vers` is the canonical family of versions of the structure, `X'` the fixed version of
 `X(x)`;
* `head` and `tail` are the color classes of `Ω`, the window of line 6 first;
* `dom` is the round of lines 7–11 on `head`, present exactly when line 6 found a dominant
 color class.

§2.4.2.  The bipartite complement of a semiregular bipartite graph is semiregular.
(Used in Cor. 2.4.32 and Prop. 2.4.30, where the paper passes "to the bipartite complement
if necessary".)

§15, line 4c: what the Extended Design Lemma asks of its input, at line 4c.

* `t_two` is *"else (: `t ≥ 2` :)"* of line 4b, the negation of line 4a's test;
* `four_t` is Theorem 10.5.1 (a), `4t < m`;
* `large_defect` is line 4c's *"now their relative symmetry defect is `≥ 1/2`"*, in the form
 Theorem 9.2.3 asks for it: *"relative symmetry defect `> 1 - α`"*.

Notation 5.2.1 (Substitution).  "For `x⃗ ∈ Ω^k`, `z ∈ Ω`, and `j ∈ [k]` we write
`x⃗^j(z)` to denote the string `y⃗ = y₁ … y_k` where `y_i = z` if `i = j` and `y_i = x_i`
if `i ≠ j`."  (Eq. (32).)

§15: **the decision tree of the seven lines**, with every test and every branch named.
This is `Babai.Sec15Inputs.step` at the round of Sec. 15, with the fields above substituted;
the seven lemmas below are the seven ways through it.

§9.4, step 9(i).  **The input of §9.6 that step 8 leaves**: the first part and the
blocks of the graph `(V₁, V₂; R_i)` of step 8, with the coloring `Babai.step8CaseColor`;
the `Case` of §9.6 is `Babai.step8_isImprimitiveCaseOn`.

The numeric parameters of the Master's node carrier, explicit. Each field replaces one
`Classical.choose` of `Algo.masterFinalOps` / `Algo.siWorkspaceRoutine`:

* `B`, `C₀`: `Algo.masterLine1B`, `Algo.masterLine1C₀` (line 1, Sec. 11.3);
* `a`, `b`: `Algo.masterCapA`, `Algo.masterCapB` (admission caps);
* `e`, `f`, `u`, `v`: `Algo.masterTopE`, `Algo.masterTopF`, `Algo.masterTopU`,
 `Algo.masterTopV` (window caps);
* `u'`, `v'`: `Algo.l57UV e f` (the window caps of the hint of lines 5–7; the round of
 lines 5–7 itself always runs with `Algo.l57UV e f`);
* `tt`, `dd`: the fuel exponent and state cap of `Algo.sbipRun` (Sec. 9) at line 4c,
 `Algo.masterLine4TT 0`, `Algo.masterLine4DD 0`;
* `t`, `d`: the same for lines 5–7 (`Algo.exists_l57Constants`);
* `dc`, `dp`: `Algo.exists_aggnPhasesOK 2`;
* `E`: `Algo.masterL57E` (reserve exponent of lines 5–7).
